#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ea56b4-2066-5aaf-aea9-08515803fc0b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression With Parentheses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bdcad11-7d8a-5421-ab94-72922785ccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bdcad11-7d8a-5421-ab94-72922785ccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446969} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e040745-c5bf-5561-ae45-d4994de16a35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c9c8087-b061-507e-9fec-13a0e9bcaffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-134224" :question/problem "Maria, Esther, and Albert each bought $1$ pound of canary seed and $3$ pounds of sunflower seeds. In total, they bought{{field-1}}\n pounds of seeds." :question/worked-solution "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of pounds of seeds.\n\nSince each of them bought a pound of canary seed and $3$ pounds of sunflower seeds, the number of pounds each person bought is:\n\n$$\n{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{canary seeds}}+{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sunflower seeds}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of seeds, and there are $3$ people, we must multiply the number of pounds of seeds by $3.$ Therefore, the total amount of seeds is:\n\n$$\n{{(1+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+3)\\times 3 & =(1+3)\\times 3 \\\\  & =4\\times 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n4\\times 3=12\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85f7df01-47df-5ca6-bdc2-9485017b51bd" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Two-Digit Number by a Two-Digit Number: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0cab70f-aaa9-5c7b-b155-83e6761a337a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0cab70f-aaa9-5c7b-b155-83e6761a337a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446971} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0cab31-0f2a-57b3-9505-dcabc68b3108" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e526d16b-2312-5be9-8a0e-4596f38c905d" :question/math-academy-id "q-134225" :question/problem "Fernando has $11$ dog food packets, each with a weight of $18\\,\\text{kg}.$ In total, Fernando has$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\text{kg}$ of dog food." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total amount of dog food, we need to multiply $11$ by $18.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}11\\times 8=88\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}11\\times 10=110\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}88+110=198\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $11\\times 18=198.$\n\nTherefore, Fernando has $198\\,\\text{kg}$ of dog food in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a1e476-39d5-529d-8632-c5dce93b8ed7" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit Is Less Than the Divisor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ec48f6-7019-59b9-9f08-9492cf165e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6ec48f6-7019-59b9-9f08-9492cf165e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446972} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aead1d3-e995-571a-b86f-f2472a7d060b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f31fa20-0ebb-52a7-bbaa-3168885484ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-134226" :question/problem "If Fernando sold all the dog food in $6$ days, and each day he sold the same amount of food, then each day he sold{{field-1}}\n$\\text{kg}$ of dog food." :question/worked-solution "Fernando sold $198\\,\\text{kg}$ of dog food in $6$ days. Since each day he sold the same amount of food, he sold $198÷6$ kilograms of food each day.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{1\\,\\phantom{|}9\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $1$ (the number of hundreds in $198$) is less than the divisor $6.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{1\\,\\phantom{|}9\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $19÷6=3\\,\\text{R}\\,1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $6\\times 3=18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $19-18=1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{1\\,\\phantom{|}9\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $18÷6=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over ones: $3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $6\\times 3=18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $18-18=0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe get the remainder $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n198÷6=33.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7bb96c-c682-54da-98d7-aa1b6960ee9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Improper Fraction and a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e98615-c052-5790-b580-33ed777619b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e98615-c052-5790-b580-33ed777619b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446968} :answer-field/id #uuid "e404a850-f14e-5356-843f-34fde6cb6d6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54d13d6c-41a6-5a8b-98ff-e263c159a957" :question/math-academy-id "q-134227" :question/problem "Leonard bought $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food and $\\frac{11}{7}$ kilograms of puppy dog food. How much dog food did Leonard buy in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nLeonard bought$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{kg}$ of dog food in total." :question/worked-solution "To find out how much dog food Leonard bought in total, we have to calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n4+\\frac{11}{7}\n$$\n\nTo add $4$ and $\\frac{11}{7},$ we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $7.$\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $7$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 7}{1\\times 7}=\\frac{28}{7}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{28+11}{7}=\\frac{39}{7}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n39÷7=5\\,\\text{R}\\,4=5\\,\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Leonard bought $5\\,\\frac{4}{7}$ $\\text{kg}$ of dog food in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee87e73-843c-5596-b1f2-341d4e7266f8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Final Answer to a Mixed Number: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92df9282-eb47-5499-b346-a3622169db36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92df9282-eb47-5499-b346-a3622169db36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446970} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbb0574-20ea-596e-8cfe-93e739d2f289" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e9a72bc-7182-50aa-b33c-312bc9872ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-134228" :question/problem "Of the $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food that Leonard bought, he gave his dog Rocky $\\frac{1}{4}$ of a kilogram. As a mixed number, Leonard has{{field-1}}\n$\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food remaining." :question/worked-solution "To find out how much adult dog food is remaining, we must calculate the difference $4-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{1}{4}$ from $4,$ we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $4.$\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 4}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{16}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{16-1}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15÷4=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Leonard had $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food remaining."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf381e5b-c6ba-5255-8d6a-797576f71baf" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions When the Least Common Denominator Equals the Product of Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4abd7043-ff82-5416-822e-a593629eeab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4abd7043-ff82-5416-822e-a593629eeab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446967} :answer-field/id #uuid "66208150-6e7d-5520-9880-3168ad2ac39f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446978} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a23b85-4778-5768-b2f8-373ac3267e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446965} {:answer/id #uuid "5b26b6cd-f914-5769-aa20-07737eddc245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b26b6cd-f914-5769-aa20-07737eddc245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500655} :answer-field/id #uuid "77c82add-3292-5a5b-b498-4577ca20c249" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "120bfde4-7193-54fa-8c9d-88126da888e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-134229" :question/problem "After Rocky ate his part of the adult dog food, the remaining amount of adult dog food is$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$than the remaining amount of puppy food because $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{11}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Leonard has $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food and  $\\frac{11}{7}\\,\\text{kg}$ of puppy dog food remaining.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $4\\times 7=28.$\n\nTo put $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ over a denominator of $28,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15\\times 7}{4\\times 7}=\\frac{105}{28}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{11}{7}$ over a denominator of $28,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{11\\times 4}{7\\times 4}=\\frac{44}{28}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{105}{28}$ and $\\frac{44}{28}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $105>44,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{105}{28}>\\frac{44}{28}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n> \"After Rocky ate his part of the adult dog food, the remaining amount of adult dog food is $>$than the remaining amount of puppy dog food because $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}>\\frac{11}{7}.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "476cf433-d982-5f14-89be-48aea2abaf55" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432d1e4f-9b09-5283-b109-100f7884965e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "432d1e4f-9b09-5283-b109-100f7884965e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446966} :answer-field/id #uuid "43dd518e-bd49-5f2f-85b6-e508e87d98d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cc5732d-71df-51a9-9fde-261bb49876c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-134230" :question/problem "Leonard gave his dog Toby $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the puppy food he bought. What **exact** amount of food did Toby eat?\n\n*Remember that Leonard bought $\\frac{11}{7}$ kilograms of puppy dog food.*\n\nToby ate$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$kilograms of food." :question/worked-solution "We know that Leonard bought $\\frac{11}{7}$ of a kilogram of puppy dog food.\n\nTo find the fraction of a kilogram that Toby ate, we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{4}$ by $\\frac{11}{7}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{1\\times 11}{4\\times 7}=\\frac{11}{28}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Toby ate $\\frac{11}{28}$ kilograms of food."}]}
